A||||4||William Federle||Cruise Clutch Sw Wiring||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||12-05-2000||08:32 PM||||I've been having problems with my clutch switch (cruise). It has been working loose and backing away from the clutch, resulting in the switch not being made and cruise control not working.<P>There are two cables plugged onto the switch. One has a yellow and a blue wire, the other has a green and a brown wire. Does anyone know what circuits are closed by these two contacts?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||12-05-2000||09:18 PM||||I can't reply to your specific question, but I had the same problem with the clutch switch for the cruise slipping.  I learned to reach under the dash while I was driving and yank the switch forward so the cruise would work.<P>The plastic threads of the switch was slipping in the bracket.  I fixed this by added a small hose clamp up against the frame which prevented the switch from sliding in the bracket.  <P>I have never had a problem with the switch since I added the hose clamp.  YOu might want to see if this will help you as well.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||12-05-2000||10:00 PM||||Thanks - the hose clamp sounds like a winner.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||12-06-2000||07:45 AM||||The other clutch switch is you starter interlock||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||12-06-2000||01:28 PM||||Yes - that's the one with the two big wires plugged into it. I was wondering about the other one. It has two sets of wires plugged into it. After I get this thing fixed where it doesn't back off, I was thinking of making a pause switch by putting a normally closed pushbutton switch in series with one of the two sets of wires from the lower clutch switch. I just can't find any information on what the four wires go to. <p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
